WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.596
Common Python security mistakes usually come from misplaced trust.

2
00:00:04.596 --> 00:00:18.068
Code accepts a value too early, relies on a default too long, assumes a caller has permission, treats a file as harmless, trusts a dependency without review, or logs data that should not be widely visible.

3
00:00:18.068 --> 00:00:23.220
These mistakes are rarely about Python being unsuitable for secure systems.

4
00:00:23.220 --> 00:00:29.084
They are about engineering decisions that need to be explicit, tested, and maintained.

5
00:00:29.084 --> 00:00:33.522
Strong Python security depends on safe APIs and clear boundaries.

6
00:00:33.522 --> 00:00:38.118
Validate input for type, shape, range, format, and business intent.

7
00:00:38.118 --> 00:00:40.575
Use parameterized database access.

8
00:00:40.575 --> 00:00:42.794
Avoid unsafe dynamic execution.

9
00:00:42.794 --> 00:00:50.243
Treat command execution, templates, parsers, uploaded files, and deserialization as high-risk boundaries.

10
00:00:50.243 --> 00:00:54.285
Enforce authorization server-side and at the object level.

11
00:00:54.285 --> 00:01:02.923
Protect tenant boundaries and sensitive operations consistently across normal, bulk, administrative, and background paths.

12
00:01:02.923 --> 00:01:09.659
Secrets, configuration, dependencies, logs, and errors are part of the product, not side concerns.

13
00:01:09.659 --> 00:01:12.511
Keep secrets out of code, images, and logs.

14
00:01:12.511 --> 00:01:17.742
Review production configuration separately from development convenience.

15
00:01:17.742 --> 00:01:21.942
Manage dependencies deliberately and keep updates testable.

16
00:01:21.942 --> 00:01:27.806
Use logs to support detection and investigation while minimizing sensitive exposure.

17
00:01:27.806 --> 00:01:33.671
Treat debug output and verbose errors as controlled diagnostics, not public behavior.

18
00:01:33.671 --> 00:01:37.791
Tools and frameworks help, but they do not replace ownership.

19
00:01:37.791 --> 00:01:49.282
The durable improvement comes from shared patterns, clear review questions, defensive tests, good defaults, careful dependency management, and operational feedback.

20
00:01:49.282 --> 00:01:51.184
The goal is not perfect code.

21
00:01:51.184 --> 00:01:58.396
The goal is to reduce repeated mistakes and make secure behavior the easiest behavior to maintain over time.
